nodecode-pass-secrets is a rewrite of dyiapanis's dyiapanis/pass-secrets:

  https://github.com/dyiapanis/pass-secrets
  commit 485f105150e09dad28461b26b9eca9ffc511b15d
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 D Yiapanis (LICENSE)

The original is one Python plugin folder for Hermes Agent: `__init__.py`
registered a `SecretSource` that walked the local pass store (a `*.gpg` file
per entry under each configured subdirectory), ran `pass show -- PATH` for
each one with a per-entry timeout, enforced UPPER_SNAKE_CASE on the leaf name
and returned the merged mapping to the orchestrator, which wrote it into the
environment; a second registration added a static note to the session prompt.
Its own part was the walk, the argv, the name rule, the error kinds
(BINARY_MISSING, NOT_CONFIGURED) and the words each one gets.

This rewrite keeps the store, the walk and the reads as a cell: `pass_list`
is the walk without a value, `pass_show` one entry's value, `pass_env` the
whole mapping the original injected, `pass_status` the prerequisite and the
counts, and `/pass` the original's own binary for anything else. The
UPPER_SNAKE_CASE name rule, `--` before every entry, the 30-second bound and
the install hints are the original's, reimplemented in Common Lisp around
`nlk:run-bounded`; `(help :pass-secrets)` is the original's README, in the
cell's voice, and `(secret NAME)` is the one-value read a form can use
instead of the environment.

Left out, and why: Hermes' secret-source seam itself, which Nodecode does not
have — so a value is read when a tool asks for it rather than injected at
startup, nothing is written into the environment, and the original's
`override_existing` precedence has nothing to act on; the system-prompt note,
because the cell's manual topic and tool descriptions say the same thing to
the same model; the fallback stub classes the original carried for imports
outside Hermes, because there is only one runtime here. `pass` and
password-store are the upstream project's; the license above covers the
plugin, not the tool.
